{
  "raw": {
    "https://ozarks.edu/wp-content/uploads/AP-Health-Promotion-August-2026.pdf": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF HEALTH PROMOTION",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF HEALTH PROMOTION \nRESPONSIBILITIES AND QUALIFICATIONS \nThe University of the Ozarks invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Health Science or \nHealth Promotion position, beginning August 2027. Candidates should be ABD or have earned a Ph.D. in \nHealth Science, Public Health, or a closely related discipline. Candidates with a master’s degree and a record \nof teaching excellence will be considered. The ideal applicant will work as part of a strong team supporting \nstudents in our Exercise Science and Health Promotion majors and minors and have an interest in rural health \noutcomes. Primary responsibilities include teaching courses focused on health promotion and education. \nCandidates who can teach some or all of the following will be given preference: Introduction to Health \nPromotion, Health Leadership, Health Management and Administration, and Health Policy. We expect the \ncandidate’s expertise will enhance the Health Promotion curriculum and encourage collaboration with students \nand colleagues to conduct research in state-of-the-art research facilities.  \n \nUniversity of the Ozarks values personal attention and collegiality, thus a familiarity with and understanding of \na small and residential campus are highly desirable.  Candidates should demonstrate a strong interest in \nworking with a culturally diverse student body. Excellent communication skills and support of the University's \nmission are also required. \n \nABOUT UNIVERSITY OF THE OZARKS \nRanked in the Top 10 of Southern region comprehensive baccalaureate institutions by U. S. News & World \nReport, University of the Ozarks is a Presbyterian-related institution that blends the liberal arts and \nprofessional preparation in a student-centered environment emphasizing quality teaching and student learning. \nThe University serves approximately 800 undergraduate students on a beautiful campus in Clarksville, \nArkansas, located along Interstate-40 approximately 90 miles northwest of Little Rock, and nestled between \nthe Arkansas River and the Ozark Mountains. The University serves students from diverse religious, cultural, \neducational and economic backgrounds and welcomes students from throughout the U.S. and from more than \n20 countries around the world. Knowing that diversity contributes to academic excellence and rich and \nrewarding communities, Ozarks is committed to recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty, staff and student \nbody. The University is especially interested in applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences and \nthose with a successful record of teaching and working with diverse students, including historically \nunderrepresented minority students and other underserved populations. Recent campaigns have enhanced \nfacilities, academic programs and other campus initiatives. For more information about University of the \nOzarks, visit www.ozarks.edu. \n \nAPPLICATION PROCEDURES \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the search is completed. Please send a \nletter of application; vita; statement of teaching philosophy; diversity statement; and three current letters of \nreference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability, to: humanresources@ozarks.edu "
    },
    "https://ozarks.edu/wp-content/uploads/AP-Religion-August-2026.pdf": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF RELIGION",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF RELIGION \nRESPONSIBILITIES AND QUALIFICATIONS \nThe University of the Ozarks invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Religion position, \nbeginning in August 2027. Candidates should be ABD or have earned a Ph.D. in Religion by the start of fall \nclasses in August 2027. Candidates with a master’s degree and a record of teaching excellence will be \nconsidered. The ideal applicant will work as part of a strong team supporting students in our Religion major \nand minor, as well as in our new Rural Church Leadership minor. We seek a teacher-scholar whose work will \nstrengthen the Religion program while contributing significantly to the University’s growing commitments to \ncommunity engagement, rural vitality, and the preparation of students for leadership in churches and other \ncommunity-serving organizations. \n \nThe position will have a distinctive connection to the Town Square Collaborative, an interdisciplinary initiative \nthat brings the University’s resources into partnership with rural communities, congregations, and community \norganizations. Working with colleagues and external partners, the successful candidate will help develop \neducational experiences that prepare students for the challenges facing rural communities and to exercise \nthoughtful, ethical, and collaborative leadership within them. \n \nPrimary responsibilities include teaching courses focused on Religion. Candidates who can teach some or all \nof the following will be given preference: Bible in American Culture, Introduction to Theology, Asian \nPhilosophies and Religions, Judaism, Christianity and Islam, and Approaches to the study of Religion. The \nteaching load for the position is 24 credits per academic year. \n \nUniversity of the Ozarks values personal attention and collegiality; thus, familiarity with and understanding of a \nsmall, residential campus are highly desirable.  Candidates should demonstrate a strong interest in working \nwith a culturally diverse student body. Excellent communication skills and support of the University's mission \nare also required. \n \nABOUT UNIVERSITY OF THE OZARKS \nRanked in the Top 10 of Southern region comprehensive baccalaureate institutions by U. S. News & World \nReport, University of the Ozarks is a Presbyterian-related institution that blends the liberal arts and \nprofessional preparation in a student-centered environment emphasizing quality teaching and student learning. \nThe University serves approximately 800 undergraduate students on a beautiful campus in Clarksville, \nArkansas, located along Interstate-40 approximately 90 miles northwest of Little Rock, and nestled between \nthe Arkansas River and the Ozark Mountains. The University serves students from diverse religious, cultural, \neducational and economic backgrounds and welcomes students from throughout the U.S. and from more than \n20 countries around the world. Knowing that diversity contributes to academic excellence and rich and \nrewarding communities, Ozarks is committed to recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty, staff and student \nbody. The University is especially interested in applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences and \nthose with a successful record of teaching and working with diverse students, including historically \nunderrepresented minority students and other underserved populations. Recent campaigns have enhanced \nfacilities, academic programs and other campus initiatives. For more information about University of the \nOzarks, visit www.ozarks.edu. \n \nPage 2 of 2 \n \nAPPLICATION PROCEDURES \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the search is completed. Please send a \nletter of application; vita; statement of teaching philosophy; diversity statement; and three current letters of \nreference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability, to: humanresources@ozarks.edu "
    },
    "https://ozarks.edu/wp-content/uploads/Assistant-Mens-Basketball-Coach-Job-Advertisement-2026.pdf": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT MEN’S BASKETBALL COACH",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nASSISTANT MEN’S BASKETBALL COACH \n \nJOB SUMMARY/OBJECTIVE: \nThis position will report to the Head Men’s Basketball Coach.  The primary duty of the Assistant Men’s Basketball Coach \nis to assist the Head Coach in all aspects of coaching and instructing team members in the knowledge of the sport.  May \nbe responsible for individual student/athlete instruction in a specific area.  Will assist in evaluating and recruiting potential \nstudent-athletes.  Included in the compensation with this position is room and board.     \n \nDUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: \n• Assist in all areas of team performance, including pre-season, post-season training, practice and competition. \n• Design and implement instructions for student-athletes in a certain area.  \n• Monitor the academic progress of student-athletes and provide encouragement, support and referral assistance \nas appropriate. \n• Assist in the day-to-day operations, including but not limited to video breakdown, travel plans, equipment and \nbudget management.  \n• Assist with identification, evaluation and recruitment of student-athletes. \n• Implement and develop fundraising strategies to support annual operating budget.  \n• Perform other duties as assigned by the Head Coach. \n• Comply with SCAC/NCAA rules and regulations and support NCAA DIII / SCAC Philosophy and Mission  \n• Promote your program (on campus, in the community, outside the community – speeches to local organizations, \nplayer volunteer work, camps, etc.). \n• Attain/Maintain CPR/AED certification. \n• Assist with playoffs (if hosting). \n• Professional Development – annually attend clinics or do other things to better your coaching ability. \n• Represent the University on and off the court in a positive fashion.   \n• Maintain appropriate relationships and communicate appropriately with Alumni, SA’s, Team, Coaching Staff, \nCommunity, and SCAC. \n• Other duties as assigned by Athletic Director. \n \nSKILLS AND ABILITIES \n• Excellent organizational ability, high level of accuracy, attention to detail. \n• Effective communication skills. \n• Able to prioritize and manage multiple tasks simultaneously and with minimal supervision.  \n• Experience working with Microsoft Office Word Pro, Excel, PowerPoint. \n• Enjoy working with college students. \n• Travel, including overnight and/or weekend. \n  \nQUALIFICATIONS: \n• Bachelor’s Degree preferred. \n• Knowledge of NCAA rules and regulations. \n• Coaching and/or playing experience at the collegiate level. \n• The successful candidate must demonstrate effective and excellent leadership, motivational, organizational and \ninterpersonal skills. \n \nUniversity of the Ozarks reserves the right to consider an equivalent combination of education, training, and/or \nexperience necessary to perform successfully the major duties and responsibilities of the position.  \n \nPage 2 of 2 \n \nAPPLICATION PROCEDURES: \nABOUT UNIVERSITY OF THE OZARKS \nRanked in the Top 10 of Southern region comprehensive baccalaureate institutions by U. S. News & World Report, \nUniversity of the Ozarks is a Presbyterian-related institution that blends the liberal arts and professional preparation in a \nstudent-centered environment emphasizing quality teaching and student learning. The University serves approximately \n800 undergraduate students on a beautiful campus in Clarksville, Arkansas, located along Interstate -40 approximately 90 \nmiles northwest of Little Rock, and nestled between the Arkansas River and the Ozark Mountains. The University serves \nstudents from diverse religious, cultural, educational and economic backgrounds and welcomes students from throughout \nthe U.S. and from more than 20 countries around the world. Knowing that diversity contributes to academic excellence \nand rich and rewarding communities, Ozarks is committed to recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty, staff and student \nbody. The University is especially interested in applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences and those with a \nsuccessful record of teaching and working with diverse students, including historically underrepresented minority students \nand other underserved populations. Recent campaigns have enhanced facilities, academic progr ams and other campus \ninitiatives. For more information about University of the Ozarks, visit www.ozarks.edu. \n \nAPPLICATION PROCEDURES \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the search is completed. Please send a \nletter of application; resume and three current letters of references to: humanresources@ozarks.edu. \n "
    },
    "https://ozarks.edu/wp-content/uploads/VPFA-2026.pdf": {
      "title": "The University of the Ozarks invites nominations, inquiries, and applications for the position of Vice President for Finance and",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nVICE PRESIDENT FOR FINANCE AND ADMINISTRATION \nRESPONSIBILITIES AND QUALIFICATIONS \nThe University of the Ozarks invites nominations, inquiries, and applications for the position of Vice President for Finance and \nAdministration (VPFA).  \nThe next VPFA will build upon the sound financial foundation of the University as reflected in its endowment and solid financ ial \nstanding, focusing on the financial management and operations of the institution to ensure fiscal stability and vitality. Rep orting \ndirectly to President Richard Dunsworth, the VPFA oversees the Office of Finance, Financial Services, Financial Aid, Human \nResources, Physical Plant, Endowment Funds, and other duties as assigned by the President. The next VPFA will join a strong \nexecutive leadership team devoted to serving the academic, spiritual, social, physical, and emotional needs of students while  \nremaining firm in its commitment to exceptionally high academic standards. The successful candida te is expected to begin no later \nthan July 1, 2027. \n \nABOUT UNIVERSITY OF THE OZARKS \nThe University of the Ozarks is a student-centered university that values honesty, service, Sabbath, hospitality, growth, and justice. \nThe institution believes that neither financial circumstances nor diverse background should be a barrier to an Ozarks edu cation. The \nUniversity promotes vigorous teaching and learning in keeping with its mission of preparing students for life beyond Ozarks, and it \nemphasizes a student experience that is academically rewarding, strengthens vocational discernment, builds personal integrity, and \nencourages spiritual growth.  \n \nRanked among the top comprehensive baccalaureate institutions in the Southern region by U.S. News & World Report, University of \nthe Ozarks blends the liberal arts and professional preparation in a student -centered environment emphasizing quality teaching and \nstudent learning. The University serves approximately 800 undergraduate students on a campus in Clarksville, Arkansas, locate d \nalong Interstate 40 approximately 90 miles northwest of Little Rock and nestled between the Arkansas River and the Ozark \nMountains. Students come from diverse religious, cultural, educational, and economic backgrounds, representing communities \nthroughout the United States and more than 20 countries around the world. Recent campaigns have enhanced facilities, academic \nprograms, and other campus initiatives. For more information, visit www.ozarks.edu.  \n \nQUALIFICATIONS \nTo perform successfully as the Vice President for Finance and Administration, the University seeks an individual able to perf orm each \nessential duty at a high professional level.  \n \nThe requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skills, and abilities required:  \n• A bachelor’s degree in a related field is required; a master’s degree in business (MBA) is preferred, and CPA licensure wil l receive \nstrong consideration \n• Understanding of and support for the University’s historic position as a faith-based institution aligned with the Presbyterian \nChurch. \n• Previous experience in a higher education setting is preferred.  \n• Experience with public or private endowments is preferred. \n• Experience leading areas of finance with oversight of financial and accounting software, preferably including knowledge of Ellucian \nColleague. \n• Strong management, organizational, and analytical skills . \n• A track record of collegiality and a demonstrated commitment to the University’s mission . \nPage 2 of 2 \n \n• An understanding of the philosophical importance of an institutional budget that focuses directly on the success of each st udent.  \n• Evidence of strong financial leadership while building consensus and securing support from key stakeholders .  \n• The ability to articulate an understanding of the University’s unique campus culture and to ensure a high degree of inclusi vity and \ntransparency. \n• Strong communication skills, with the ability to translate complex financial issues into understandable language.  \n \nAPPLICATION & NOMINATION PROCESS \nAcademic Search is assisting The University of the Ozarks in this search. Conversations are encouraged as our consultants wan t to \nget to know you, to share information that might be helpful in discerning your interest and preparing application materials, and to \nassist as you navigate every step of the application and interview process. Inquiries, nominations, and applications are invi ted. \nNominations should be sent electronically to OzarksVPFA@academicsearch.org.  \n \nPlease arrange a confidential discussion by contacting: \nDr. Alan Cureton      Ms. Disa Mason \n                                                         Senior Consultant               Consultant \n                               alan.cureton@academicsearch.org    disa.mason@academicsearch.org \n                                                         Direct booking link: Book here \n                                                         612-281-0094  \n \nThis position is open until filled, but only applications received by January 4, 2027, can be assured full consideration. Early \napplication submission is recommended, as the screening committee will review materials as they are received and utilize an \naccelerated interview process for candidates of interest. Applications should be submitted to Academic Search using this link : . \nApplications should consist of 1) a detailed letter of interest that describes the candidate’s interest and alignment with th e \nleadership agenda, 2) a current curriculum vitae, and 3) a list of five professional references with current email contact in formation. \nReferences will not be contacted without the permission of the candidate, and only for those invited to interview. All documents \nshould be uploaded to the Application Portal via the link above in PDF format. Knowing that diversity contributes to academic  \nexcellence and rich and rewarding communities, Ozarks is committed to recru iting and retaining a diverse faculty, staff and student \nbody. The University is especially interested in applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences and those with a success ful \nrecord of teaching and working with diverse students, including historically underrepresented minority students and other \nunderserved populations. For additional information about The University of the Ozarks, please visit https://ozarks.edu/ \n \n \n \n"
    },
    "https://ozarks.edu/wp-content/uploads/AP-Info-Science-2026.pdf": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF INFORMATION SCIENCE",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF INFORMATION SCIENCE \nRESPONSIBILITIES AND QUALIFICATIONS \nThe University of the Ozarks invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Information Science \nposition, beginning in August 2027. Candidates should be ABD or have earned a Ph.D. in Information Science \nor a related field by the start of fall classes in August 2027. Candidates with a master’s degree and a record of \nteaching excellence will be considered. We seek an innovative teacher-scholar who will help develop and \nstrengthen the curriculum in information science and prepare students to use data, technology, and information \nresponsibly and effectively in a wide range of professional and community settings. \n \nThe successful candidate will teach introductory and advanced courses in information science and contribute \nto interdisciplinary programs across the College. Areas of specialization are open, with particular interest in \ncandidates whose teaching and scholarship engage one or more of the following areas: data science and \nanalytics; database design and management; human-computer interaction; information visualization; \ngeographic information systems; artificial intelligence and machine learning; or information ethics and policy. \nWe especially welcome candidates who can help students connect technical knowledge with the human \ncontexts in which information is created, organized, interpreted, and used. The successful candidate will be \ncommitted to accessible and inclusive undergraduate teaching and able to engage students with varied levels \nof technical preparation. The teaching load is 24 credit hours per academic year. \n \nThe University of the Ozarks values personal attention and collegiality; thus, familiarity with and understanding \nof a small, residential campus are highly desirable.  Candidates should demonstrate a strong interest in \nworking with a culturally diverse student body. Excellent communication skills and support of the University's \nmission are also required. \n \nABOUT UNIVERSITY OF THE OZARKS \nRanked in the Top 10 of Southern region comprehensive baccalaureate institutions by U. S. News & World \nReport, University of the Ozarks is a Presbyterian-related institution that blends the liberal arts and \nprofessional preparation in a student-centered environment emphasizing quality teaching and student learning. \nThe University serves approximately 800 undergraduate students on a beautiful campus in Clarksville, \nArkansas, located along Interstate-40 approximately 90 miles northwest of Little Rock, and nestled between \nthe Arkansas River and the Ozark Mountains. The University serves students from diverse religious, cultural, \neducational and economic backgrounds and welcomes students from throughout the U.S. and from more than \n20 countries around the world. Knowing that diversity contributes to academic excellence and rich and \nrewarding communities, Ozarks is committed to recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty, staff and student \nbody. The University is especially interested in applicants with diverse backgrounds and experiences and \nthose with a successful record of teaching and working with diverse students, including historically \nunderrepresented minority students and other underserved populations. Recent campaigns have enhanced \nfacilities, academic programs and other campus initiatives. For more information about University of the \nOzarks, visit www.ozarks.edu. \n \nAPPLICATION PROCEDURES \nPage 2 of 2 \n \nReview of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the search is completed. Please send a \nletter of application; vita; statement of teaching philosophy; diversity statement; and three current letters of \nreference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability, to: humanresources@ozarks.edu "
    },
    "https://ozarks.edu/wp-content/uploads/Public-Safety-Officer-Overnight-Weekend-2026.pdf": {
      "title": "Working under the supervision of the Director of Public Safety, the officer patrols campus, monitors access -control",
      "description": " \n \n \n \n \nPUBLIC SAFETY OFFICER, OVERNIGHT & WEEKEND SHIFT \n \nSummary \nThe Public Safety Officer provides overnight and weekend safety and security coverage for the University of the Ozarks. \nWorking under the supervision of the Director of Public Safety, the officer patrols campus, monitors access -control \nsystems, responds to calls for assistance, documents incidents, and reports safety or security concerns. The officer \nassists students, employees, and visitors; coordinates with local law enforcement and emergency services when needed; \nand follows University procedures while exercising sound judgment during assigned shifts. This full-time, 12-month \nposition works Friday, Saturday, and Sunday nights from 7:00 p.m. to 7:00 a.m.  \n \nPublic Safety Officer Duties and Responsibilities: \nThis position is scheduled to work Friday, Saturday, and Sunday nights from 7:00 p.m. to 7:00 a.m. The officer must \nremain on campus until properly relieved at the end of the assigned shift. \n \nPublic Safety Officer duties include but are not limited to: \n• Efficiency and knowledge of all Public Safety information systems. \n• Monitoring electronic locking software. \n• Making rounds and checking all campus buildings at regular intervals.  \n• Maintaining call and computerized duty logs as assigned. \n• Completing incident reports and daily logs. \n• Performing safety inspections as assigned & reporting issues. \n• Offering assistance to students and staff \no Supplying escort on campus or immediate surrounding area when requested.  \no Unlocking doors for individuals with proper identification and pass or prior approval.  \no Rendering first aid and call Police or 911 when necessary. \n• Acting as liaison between law enforcement and the University when on duty.  \n• Issuing vehicle parking citations. \n• Checking University vehicles in and out in accordance with established procedures.  \n• Maintaining a strong working relationship with local law enforcement and emergency services.  \n• Other duties as assigned. \n \nSkills and Abilities \n• Willingness to learn \n• Ability to work with others and independently \n• Flexibility \n• Strong communication skills \n• Compassionate \n \nQualifications: \n  Required: \n• High School diploma or equivalent \n• First aid with CPR training (must be obtained within six weeks of hire) \n  Preferred:   \n• Experience working in a university environment \n \nApplication Process: Applications can be picked up and returned to the Public Safety Office:  Seay Student Center or \nemailed to HumanResources@ozarks.edu .   "
    }
  },
